  body: "# Industrial Injuries scheme components and boundaries\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\nThe source lists
    IIDB, US, CAA, ESDA, REA, RA and retained old/death-benefit cases as different components, with IIDB linked
    to loss of faculty from an industrial accident or prescribed disease.\n\n## Scope and exceptions\n\nThe opening
    paragraph calls the scheme non-contributory but its next numbered phrase says “no fault contributory”; this
    internal wording inconsistency is retained as a source-owner question. Component introductions include closed
    routes and dated conditions, not a current menu of new claims.\n\n## Inspected passages\n\n- [DMG 64001, PDF
    page 2](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/664b1c4dbd01f5ed32793e51/dmg-chapter-64-background-to-the-industrial-scheme.pdf#page=2).\n\
    - [DMG 64007, PDF page 3](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/664b1c4dbd01f5ed32793e51/dmg-chapter-64-background-to-the-industrial-scheme.pdf#page=3).\n\
    - [DMG 64012, PDF page 3](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/664b1c4dbd01f5ed32793e51/dmg-chapter-64-background-to-the-industrial-scheme.pdf#page=3).\n\
    \n## Additional context\n\n- [Context: chapter 64, PDF page 5](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/664b1c4dbd01f5ed32793e51/dmg-chapter-64-background-to-the-industrial-scheme.pdf#page=5).\n\
    \n## Evidence and review status\n\nIndependent experimental interpretation of captured DWP guidance, not an
    official DWP document. Definitions and semantic associations are model-assisted proposals awaiting specialist
    review. No individual entitlement decision, medical conclusion, award calculation or executable rule is provided.\n\
    \nSource observation, listing-page update, printed revision, legal event and claimant-event dates are separate.
    Current legal applicability is unresolved. Page numbers are one-based PDF positions; machine extraction may
    damage reading order, characters and footnotes. No visual PDF or specialist acceptance is claimed.\n\n- `dmg-vol11-ch64`:
    PDF `6397b92476509f66c98cbd574ef4692bf1888e41fe7da4a9ec227b7a13a62b78`; extracted pages `2bbe57d32fca42027ea6a81e508bda907df0d56da34c4d59ba73862530a659e8`;
    acquired `2026-09-15T19:45:33.768652Z`.\n"
  resource:
    https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/664b1c4dbd01f5ed32793e51/dmg-chapter-64-background-to-the-industrial-scheme.pdf#page=2
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/iidb-loss-of-faculty
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The overview links IIDB to loss of faculty arising from accident or prescribed disease.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol11-ch64/0003
      quote: "64012 IIDB is the main benefit in the scheme. It provides for people who suffer a loss of faculty
        because\nof an IA or a PD."
      locator: DMG 64012
  - target: term/rea-earning-capacity
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: REA is identified as a distinct component with its own loss-of-earnings and historical
      boundaries.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol11-ch64/0004
      quote: "64029 A person may be entitled to REA if they are assessed as 1% disabled or more because of their\n\
        injury or disease and they cannot go back to their normal job or one which pays as much. The accident or\n\
        date of onset must be before 1.10.90. In addition a person cannot be entitled to REA in respect of any\n\
        diseases prescribed or any extension to the prescriptions of existing diseases on or after 10.10.94."
      locator: DMG 64029
  - target: term/industrial-old-scheme-transition
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The overview identifies old scheme cases separately and scopes them to claims before 5 December
      2012.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol11-ch64/0004
      quote: 64037 Old scheme cases are claims made before 5.12.12 under the
      locator: DMG 64037
